WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:06.560
My name is Miranda Cheng and I do research
here in the University of Amsterdam on the

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.610
boundary between physics and mathematics.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:14.150
I use mathematics to study the physical properties
of various systems.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:20.619
And, in the other direction, I use physical
insights to try to think about mathematical

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:21.759
questions.

00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:27.550
Umbral moonshine is a fascinating relation
between finites groups on the one hand and

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.839
mock modular forms on the other hand.

00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:36.810
Finite groups are structres capturing the
symmetry properties of various subjects.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:43.720
So, repeating systems and patterns in 24 dimensions
in this case.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.800
And mock modular forms?

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:53.579
Well, they’re really cute functions displaying
very funny properties under modular transformation,

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:59.239
and in the meanwhile they have been found
to have relations to other branches of mathematics

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:03.550
such as combinatorics and so on.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.310
And what I hope umbral moonshine can teach
us?

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:12.210
Well, the most exciting prospect for me is
the following: I hope it will open a

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:18.330
window for us into some fundamental aspects
of string theory, which is a theory that we

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:25.670
believe to capture the physics at the most
fundamental level in our world.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:33.909
In particular, I hope that it will teach us
something about how quantum states allow by supersymmetry

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:37.309
can organize themselves in a specific way.

